G15.1 — a slim arming-test target, so 0007's tests EXECUTE

WHAT THIS FIXES. `0007` added three tests to shorebird_unittests.cc pinning the
Route B arming contract, and they had never run. shorebird_unittests also compiles
patch_cache_unittests.cc and deps //flutter/runtime/shorebird:patch_cache, whose
patch_cache.cc calls Shorebird_ReadLinkHeader — a symbol only Shorebird's private
Dart fork defines. So on this host build there was an object file
(shorebird_unittests.shorebird_unittests.o) and NO linked binary at
out/host_release_arm64/shorebird_unittests. A compile earns nothing under PARITY's
Status vocabulary, so the contract was pinned on paper.

WHY DROPPING THE DEP IS LEGITIMATE AND NOT A WORKAROUND. source_set("shorebird")
does not depend on patch_cache, and //flutter/runtime gates that dep on
shorebird_use_interpreter, declared `is_ios` in flutter/common/config.gni and not
overridden in this host build's args.gn — so it is false here and the symbol is
genuinely unnecessary. MEASURED, not argued: the target links (257/257) and the
three tests pass, which is what rules out the alternative that the symbol arrives
through //flutter/runtime anyway (in which case the honest move was to build the
tests in the shipping iOS tree instead).

The target must stay INSIDE the `if (enable_unittests)` block: at top level
`:shorebird_fixtures` is an undefined label and gn errors.

THE ROOT THIS WAS GENERATED FROM, because the series is inconsistent about it:
`git diff` run in /Volumes/build/route-b/flutter, the monorepo root, so paths read
`engine/src/flutter/shell/common/shorebird/BUILD.gn`. That matches 0003's prefix
style and NOT 0007's, which is relative to engine/src/flutter.

  apply:  git apply -p1        from /Volumes/build/route-b/flutter
     or:  patch -p3            from /Volumes/build/route-b/flutter/engine/src/flutter

VERIFY BY CONTENT, NEVER BY `git apply` EXIT CODE (create_checkout.sh:58 says why):

  grep -n 'shorebird_arming_unittests' \
    engine/src/flutter/shell/common/shorebird/BUILD.gn        # the target exists
  ninja -C out/host_release_arm64 shorebird_arming_unittests  # it links
  ./out/host_release_arm64/shorebird_arming_unittests \
    --gtest_filter='Shorebird.RouteBArming*'                  # [ PASSED ] 3 tests

Measured 2026-08-13: linked, and RouteBArmingIsInertWithoutAPatch,
RouteBArmingInstallsACallbackForAPatch, RouteBArmingChainsAnExistingCallback all
pass. This says nothing about two engines — that is G15's own gate and needs a host
that constructs two FlutterEngines in one process.

